# MovieNight Backend Rules

- Target .NET 10 and ASP.NET Core Web API.
- Keep a single backend application project unless an explicit project decision changes this.
- Organize business code primarily by feature.
- Keep TMDB transport concerns under the TMDB integration boundary.
- Never expose raw TMDB response models as MovieNight public API contracts.
- The frontend must obtain movie data through the MovieNight API, not directly from TMDB.
- Feature services may use EF Core `DbContext` directly; do not add generic repositories or custom Unit of Work abstractions.
- Do not introduce MediatR, CQRS, AutoMapper, Redis, message brokers, background-job infrastructure, or authentication frameworks without explicit approval.
- Use ASP.NET Core Problem Details for public API errors rather than leaking raw exceptions or third-party responses.
- Keep secrets and API keys out of source files and committed configuration.
- Use `IHttpClientFactory`-managed HTTP clients for TMDB integration.
- Use `IMemoryCache` only for clearly useful repeated TMDB reads; do not create complex cache infrastructure.
- Prefer explicit mapping and straightforward code over generic frameworks for this project.
- Add tests where business rules or API failure behavior can regress meaningfully.
- Preserve cancellation and asynchronous I/O where appropriate for HTTP/database operations.
